{0 osm: OpenStreetMap services in OCaml}

[osm] provides typed Overpass and Nominatim clients using Eio, Fetch and Jsont.
Link the [osm] library. For the example below also link [fetch-httpz], [eio]
and [eio_main]. The source uses OxCaml's portability annotations and requires
an OxCaml toolchain compatible with its dependencies.

{1:start Quick start}

{[
let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.Time.with_timeout_exn env#clock 30. @@ fun () ->
  let client = Osm.v (Fetch_httpz.std env) in
  match Osm.Nominatim.search client ~q:"Cambridge, UK" ~limit:3 () with
  | Error error -> Format.eprintf "%a@." Osm.pp_error error
  | Ok places ->
      List.iter (fun (place : Osm.Place.t) ->
        Printf.printf "%s (%g, %g)\n"
          place.display_name place.point.lat place.point.lon) places
]}

The defaults are private service URLs, not public OpenStreetMap infrastructure:
{ul
{- [http://marpe:8888/nominatim/] for search and reverse geocoding.}
{- [http://marpe:8888/overpass/api/] for interpreter queries and status.}}

Pass [~nominatim_url] and [~overpass_url] to {!Osm.v} to use other services.
Supply base URLs, not operation paths. HTTP and HTTPS are accepted. Fetch owns
TLS, credentials, retries and rate limits. The library adds none of those
policies. Configure them on the Fetch client as required by your service.

{1:queries Typed queries}

{[
let nearby_cafes client point =
  let open Osm in
  let cafes = Query.select Nwr
      ~scope:(Query.around ~radius:500. point)
      ~filters:[Query.eq "amenity" "cafe"] in
  Overpass.run client (Query.elements ~geometry:Center [cafes])

let count_trees client place =
  let open Osm in
  match Place.area place with
  | None -> invalid_arg "An area needs a way or relation"
  | Some reference ->
      let trees = Query.select Nwr ~scope:(Query.area reference)
          ~filters:[Query.eq "natural" "tree"] in
      Overpass.run client (Query.count [trees])
]}

An element query returns {!Osm.Dataset.t}. A count query returns
{!Osm.Element.count}. The abstract {!Osm.Query.t} ties a query to its result
codec. Filters in one selection are conjunctive. Multiple selections form a
union. {!Osm.Query.to_ql} exposes the generated text.

Area queries use [map_to_area]. The server must have generated an area for the
source way or relation. A source with no area matches nothing. A missing area
dispatcher is a service error. Radius queries use metres. Coordinates use
latitude and longitude in degrees, never GeoJSON's longitude-first array order.

Use {!Osm.Overpass.query} for complete QL outside the builder's subset. Include
an explicit JSON output directive, for example:
{v [out:json][timeout:10];node(around:50,52.2053,0.1218);out 3; v}
Use {!Osm.Overpass.query_raw} for XML or CSV responses. The library does not
parse those formats.

{1:results Reading results}

{[
let inspect result =
  List.iter (function
    | Osm.Element.Node node ->
        Printf.printf "Node %Ld\n" node.common.id
    | Osm.Element.Way way ->
        (match Osm.Dataset.way_nodes result way with
        | Ok nodes -> Printf.printf "%d way nodes\n" (List.length nodes)
        | Error Osm.Dataset.Not_requested -> print_endline "Node IDs omitted"
        | Error (Osm.Dataset.Missing refs) ->
            Printf.printf "%d referenced nodes missing\n" (List.length refs))
    | _ -> ()) (Osm.Dataset.elements result)
]}

Objects are immutable decoded records. Pattern matching distinguishes nodes,
ways, relations, areas, counts and unknown element types. A missing list is
[None], while a requested empty list is [Some []]. Cropped geometry retains
null entries. A center is a bounding-box center, not necessarily a point on
the feature or an address.

Traversal performs no I/O. {!Osm.Dataset.find} indexes by kind and ID, since
different kinds can share a numeric ID. Way traversal preserves order and
repeated closing nodes. Relation traversal preserves roles and stops at direct
members. Use {!Osm.Query.references} for explicit follow-up requests, or request
all descendants with [Query.elements ~recurse:true]. Output limits may still
omit referenced objects.

{1:codecs Codecs and round-trips}

The public [jsont] values work without an HTTP client and compose with Jsont
objects and lists:

{[
let decode_places text =
  Jsont_bytesrw.decode_string (Jsont.list Osm.Place.jsont) text
]}

Link [jsont] and [jsont.bytesrw] for this example. Coordinates, bounds and IDs
are validated during decoding. Construct coordinates with {!Osm.Point.v},
bounds with {!Osm.Bounds.v}, and references with {!Osm.Reference.v}.

Place, element and dataset codecs retain and replay their complete raw JSON,
including unknown fields and attribution. Their records are private so typed
fields cannot diverge from raw data. Smaller value codecs encode their known
fields and may discard unknown fields. Count-query encoding produces a minimal
envelope containing the count element, not the original response metadata.
OSM tag keys and address categories remain extensible maps.

{!Osm.Dataset.jsont} maps data and preserves remarks. {!Osm.Overpass.decode}
also rejects API errors and nonempty remarks, including partial timeout results.
The HTTP entry points use this stricter behavior.

{1:errors Errors and resource ownership}

Each call owns and closes its HTTP response. Calls need an Eio runtime, but
client construction opens no connections. HTTP statuses, decoding failures,
API errors and response limits return {!Osm.error}. Transport failures and
cancellation propagate as exceptions. Invalid caller arguments raise
[Invalid_argument] before a request is sent.

Responses default to a 16 MiB byte limit. This bounds wire data read into
memory, not the total allocation of decoded objects. No overall deadline is
installed. Use [Eio.Time.with_timeout_exn] around a call. The builder's timeout
is a server execution limit, not a client network deadline.

{1:api API}

{!modules:Osm}
